# The folio keyboard's platform device exposes its status files under
# /sys/devices/platform/soc/soc:lenovo_kb/. Stock gives that subtree its own
# type rather than leaving it as plain sysfs, and the keyboard HAL is the only
# thing that reads and writes it.
type sysfs_kb, fs_type, sysfs_type;

# The stylus and folio knobs the Lenovo touchscreen HAL drives:
#   /proc/pen_type          which stylus generation is attached
#   /proc/pen_attached      attach/detach state
#   /proc/pen_wakeup_mode   whether a stylus event may wake the panel
#   /proc/folio_case_mode   folio open/closed, mirrored from the keyboard
#
# Stock leaves these as plain "proc" and simply lets its HAL write any proc
# file. Giving them a type of their own means the HAL can be granted exactly
# these four and nothing else, and it also makes them reachable from other
# domains later (a Settings-side stylus toggle, say) without handing that
# domain the whole of /proc.
type proc_lenovo_pen, fs_type, proc_type;

# The touch-tuning knobs the same HAL drives. Split from proc_lenovo_pen only
# because they are a different feature area, not a different privilege level:
#
#   /proc/panel_direction   panel rotation the touch driver maps coordinates to
#   /proc/game_mode         low-latency touch mode
#   /proc/game_edge         per-edge rejection while in game mode
#   /proc/edge_grid_zone    grip/edge rejection zones
#   /proc/report_threshold  touch report threshold
#   /proc/HighReportRate    high touch report rate
#   /proc/haptics           touch-driven haptics passthrough
#   /proc/gesture_mode      gesture wake
#
# Only the pen four were labelled here originally, so the rest stayed
# u:object_r:proc:s0 and every access was denied: no stylus tuning, no game
# mode, no edge rejection, no report-rate control, no panel rotation for touch.
# Stock gets away with plain "proc" because its policy grants
# hal_lenovotouchscreen_default proc:file outright.
#
# ⚠️ The set used to be taken from the stock
# vendor/etc/init/vendor.lenovo.hardware.touchscreen-service.rc, which chowns
# twelve paths. That was the wrong source: the .rc is written for a whole SKU
# line, so it chowns /proc/gesture_control -- a node this unit does not have
# (verified: `ls -Z /proc/gesture_control` -> No such file) -- and it never
# chowns /proc/gesture_mode or /proc/support_pen, which the HAL really does use.
# The old note here even recorded that gesture_control is absent and called its
# genfscon entry "harmless"; what it missed is that the real node exists under a
# different name and was left unlabelled, so the denial remained.
#
# The authority is the HAL binary:
#   strings vendor/bin/hw/vendor.lenovo.hardware.touchscreen-service \
#     | grep '^/proc/'      -> 13 paths, all of them real on this unit
#
# /proc/support_pen is grouped with the pen nodes (proc_lenovo_pen) since that
# is its feature area. Both it and gesture_mode are root:root 0666 on stock --
# the .rc never chowns them -- so DAC is not the gate; SELinux is.
type proc_lenovo_touch, fs_type, proc_type;

# ⚠️ vendor_persist_audio_file is NOT declared here. The QTI vendor policy
# already defines it (device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/
# file.te:174 and its siblings), and re-declaring it is a hard checkpolicy
# error, "Duplicate declaration of type". This tree only needs the
# file_contexts entry that points /mnt/vendor/persist/factory/audio at it and
# the grant in hal_audio.te; the type itself comes from upstream.
#
# What that directory is: the Awinic amplifiers' per-unit calibration blob
# (aw_cali.bin), written once on the production line and read by PAL inside
# hal_audio_default at every HAL start. Using the existing type rather than the
# generic mnt_vendor_file is what keeps the audio HAL out of the rest of
# persist, where the Bluetooth and Wi-Fi MAC blobs and secnvm live.

# /sys/.../aw882xx_chipid — the smart-amplifier chip id nodes exported by
# aw882xx.ko, one per i2c address (5-0034 .. 5-0037). Stock leaves them as plain
# sysfs and lets the audio HAL read all of sysfs; giving them a type means the
# grant in hal_audio.te names exactly these.
type sysfs_awinic, fs_type, sysfs_type;

# /sys/class/qcom-battery — see genfs_contexts. Split out of plain sysfs so the
# charging-control gate can be opened from init.malbec.rc with a rule that names
# exactly this class and nothing else.
type sysfs_qcom_battery, fs_type, sysfs_type;
